UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 18: Nutrition and health', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Water is the everyday drink for hydration. Thirst, heat, and activity increase fluid needs. Sugary drinks add energy without the same hydration-first habit. Sports drinks are not required for ordinary school sport for most students — adults advise for special cases.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose the better everyday hydration plan for a school day: water bottle refilled, or only soft drink at lunch?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA refilled water bottle is the everyday hydration plan; soft drink alone is not.\n\nWATCH FOR\nWaiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything. Regular sips prevent the crash.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a hydration plan for a hot sports carnival day with timing (before / during / after) using water. One sentence on why soft drink is not the main plan.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Food Technology","learning_intention":"Water is the everyday drink for hydration. Thirst, heat, and activity increase fluid needs. Sugary drinks add energy without the same hydration-first habit. Sports drinks are not required for ordinary school sport for most students — adults advise for special cases.","lesson_number":18,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["FT5-NUT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a hydration plan for a hot sports carnival day with timing (before / during / after) using water. One sentence on why soft drink is not the main plan. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using hydration on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything.. Correction: A refilled water bottle is the everyday hydration plan;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything. Regular sips prevent the crash. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating hydration on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with hydration, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about hydration with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for hydration. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for hydration. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A refilled water bottle is the everyday hydration plan","Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work"],"question":"Choose the better everyday hydration plan for a school day: water bottle refilled, or only soft drink at lunch?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for hydration; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of water with clear evidence","Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything","Water is the everyday drink for hydration","A refilled water bottle is the everyday hydration plan"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named water, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything","Name water only, without linking it to hydration","Apply hydration to new material and name the water used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies hydration with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on hydration. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A refilled water bottle is the everyday hydration plan","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves hydration","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Water is the everyday drink for hydration.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","On a warm PE day, carrying a water bottle and drinking across the afternoon supports concentration better than waiting"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything","Name water only, with no linked example","Write a hydration plan for a hot sports carnival day with timing (before / during / after) using water","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies hydration to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about hydration with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating hydration on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using hydration on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for hydration. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with hydration, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything.. Correction: A refilled water bottle is the everyday hydration plan;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-food-technology-y9-w13-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 alignment. Food Technology 7–10 (2025) Stage 4 may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (FT4-ADJ-01). Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Not medical advice. Practical cooking optional and adult-supervised.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise hydration.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Food Technology","success_criteria":["I can use hydration with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify water and explain why it matters.","I can practise thirst independently on a new prompt.","I can notice activity needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Water is the everyday drink for hydration.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Water is the everyday drink for hydration","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOn a warm PE day, carrying a water bottle and drinking across the afternoon supports concentration better than waiting until a headache arrives. A large soft drink may feel cold but is not the same hydration habit as water.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose the better everyday hydration plan for a school day: water bottle refilled, or only soft drink at lunch?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA refilled water bottle is the everyday hydration plan; soft drink alone is not.","explain":"Water is the everyday drink for hydration. Thirst, heat, and activity increase fluid needs. Sugary drinks add energy without the same hydration-first habit. Sports drinks are not required for ordinary school sport for most students — adults advise for special cases.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything. Regular sips prevent the crash. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Two food choices side by side for the lesson idea.","caption":"Compare using the taught nutrition idea.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Idea</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">A</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Compare</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">B</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: On a warm PE day, carrying a water bottle and drinking across the afternoon supports concentration better than waiting u Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: On a warm PE day, carrying a water bottle and drinking across the afternoon supports concentration better than waiting u Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOn a warm PE day, carrying a water bottle and drinking across the afternoon supports concentration better than waiting until a headache arrives. A large soft drink may feel cold but is not the same hydration habit as water. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Use evidence from labels or habits before deciding.","caption":"Not medical advice — classroom food literacy.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Check</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">label/habit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Decide</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">everyday pattern</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWaiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything. Regular sips prevent the crash. Here is a more productive model: On a warm PE day, carrying a water bottle and drinking across the afternoon supports concentration better than waiting until a headache arrives. A large soft drink may feel cold but is not the same hydration habit as water.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nA refilled water bottle is the everyday hydration plan; soft drink alone is not.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Waiting for extreme thirst before drinking anything. Regular sips prevent the crash. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hydration?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWater is the everyday drink for hydration. Thirst, heat, and activity increase fluid needs. Sugary drinks add energy without the same hydration-first habit. Sports drinks are not required for ordinary school sport for most students — adults advise for special cases. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 18: Nutrition and health","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Nutrition and health","week":14,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nOn a warm PE day, carrying a water bottle and drinking across the afternoon supports concentration better than waiting until a headache arrives. A large soft drink may feel cold but is not the same hydration habit as water.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two food choices side by side for the lesson idea.","caption":"Compare using the taught nutrition idea.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Idea</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">A</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Compare</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">B</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Use evidence from labels or habits before deciding.","caption":"Not medical advice — classroom food literacy.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Check</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">label/habit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Decide</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">everyday pattern</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-food-technology-y9-w13-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
